<h1>Tuval — Cardiac &amp; Metabolic Review</h1>
<div class="subtitle">Built from your 14-year lab history (2012 → May 2026) · Maccabi · LabCorp · Sheba Longevity · Cyprus · Built 2026-05-16</div>

<div class="intro">
  <b>Tuvalito —</b> Bez asked me (his AI medical analyst) to go through your labs. I read everything from 2012 through May 2026 — every Maccabi panel, your Sheba Longevity assessment, the Cyprus draw. Here's the honest read. Some things are excellent. <b>Some things need attention this month.</b> All of this belongs in a conversation with your cardiologist — I'm a pattern-finder, not a prescriber.
</div>

<div class="section">🚨 The Big Picture in One Paragraph</div>

<p>You have a stent in place from a decade-plus ago, and your labs show that <b>the underlying disease that caused the stent is not adequately controlled.</b> Your LDL is double the post-stent target. Your ApoB (the best risk marker we have) is dangerously high at 136. Your Lp(a) is 60 — a genetic high-risk marker you can't change but can offset with aggressive treatment. Your kidneys are leaking protein (chronic albuminuria) which signals vascular damage. Your ascending aorta is enlarging (4.5 cm). <b>None of this is an emergency tonight, but all of it means your current statin dose is undertreating you.</b> The good news: your heart pump function is excellent, your stress test is great, bone density is strong, no diabetes, cognition is normal. The fixes are mostly conventional — just need to be done.</p>

<div class="section">🔴 The 5 Urgent Items — Bring to Your Cardiologist This Month</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>#</th><th>Finding</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>Action</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="urgent-tag">1</span></td>
    <td><b>LDL 108 (May 2026)</b><br>Post-stent target: &lt;55 mg/dL<br>ApoB 136 (Sep 2024) · target &lt;60</td>
    <td>You are on Crestor 15 mg, which is sub-therapeutic for a stent patient. ApoB at 136 means your atherogenic particle load is roughly 2× what it needs to be. This is the single biggest driver of another cardiac event.</td>
    <td>Crestor 15 → 40 mg OR add ezetimibe 10 mg OR add a PCSK9 inhibitor (Repatha / Praluent). Goal: LDL &lt;55, ApoB &lt;60. Re-check labs in 8 weeks.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="urgent-tag">2</span></td>
    <td><b>Lp(a) 60 mg/dL</b><br>Target: &lt;30. Tested twice (2019: 46, 2024: 60)</td>
    <td>Lp(a) is genetic — you were born with this and it never changes. It's a major hidden cause of premature CAD. At 60, you have ~2× the cardiac risk of someone with normal Lp(a). Cannot be lowered with current standard meds — but the harm is offset by aggressive LDL/ApoB lowering.</td>
    <td>Ask cardiologist about Lp(a)-targeted therapies in trials (pelacarsen, olpasiran). Otherwise, the answer is: push LDL even lower than standard (target &lt;40 mg/dL).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="urgent-tag">3</span></td>
    <td><b>Chronic albuminuria</b><br>UACR trending 49 → 225 → 191 mg/g<br>(Target &lt;30)</td>
    <td>Your kidneys have been leaking protein for years. This is a sign of vascular/diabetic damage to small kidney vessels. It's also an independent predictor of cardiovascular events. Your Losartan 50 mg is helping but not enough.</td>
    <td>Two things: (a) maximize Losartan to 100 mg (or switch to a stronger ARB), (b) <b>add an SGLT2 inhibitor (empagliflozin or dapagliflozin)</b> — has strong data for proteinuria reduction AND cardiac event reduction, even in non-diabetics. This is the modern standard.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="urgent-tag">4</span></td>
    <td><b>Ascending aorta 4.5 cm</b><br>(Feb 2026 Assuta echo)<br>Surveillance threshold: 4.0 cm</td>
    <td>Your aorta has dilated past the watch-only threshold. Risk of dissection rises sharply &gt;5.0 cm. Need surveillance schedule established and tight BP control.</td>
    <td>Confirm with cardiologist: (a) repeat echo or CT aorta in 6 months not 12, (b) BP target tightened to &lt;125/75, (c) avoid heavy lifting with breath-holding (Valsalva). At 5.0 cm referral to vascular surgeon for monitoring.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="urgent-tag">5</span></td>
    <td><b>Stage 2 hypertension</b><br>144/73 (Sheba Oct 2025)<br>Most recent home: ?</td>
    <td>144 systolic with an enlarging aorta is a bad combination. Norvasc 2.5 + Losartan 50 is a starter regimen, not optimized.</td>
    <td>Push Losartan to 100, push Norvasc to 5 or 10. Get a home BP cuff and measure morning + evening for 1 week. Bring averages to cardiology visit. Target &lt;125/75 given the aorta.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="section">🟡 The 4 Moderate Items — Bring to Your PCP / GI / Endo</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>#</th><th>Finding</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>Action</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="moderate-tag">6</span></td>
    <td><b>H. pylori positive</b><br>(GI-Map stool, Mar 2023 — untreated since)</td>
    <td>H. pylori is a stomach bacterial infection. Untreated, it causes chronic gastritis, ulcers, and is a class I carcinogen for stomach cancer. Three years is a long time to leave it.</td>
    <td>Standard triple therapy: PPI (omeprazole 20 mg 2×/day) + clarithromycin 500 mg 2×/day + amoxicillin 1 g 2×/day for 14 days. Or quadruple therapy. Confirm eradication with stool antigen 4–8 weeks after treatment.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="moderate-tag">7</span></td>
    <td><b>Pre-diabetes drifting</b><br>Fasting glucose 115 (May 2026) — above 100 cutoff<br>HbA1c 5.5–5.9 over time</td>
    <td>Glucose 115 is impaired fasting glucose. HbA1c trend bouncing between borderline and normal. Combined with the albuminuria, the kidney damage suggests this has been silently active for years.</td>
    <td>Same SGLT2 inhibitor as #3 hits this too. Also: weight loss (BMI 31.2), Mediterranean/Esselstyn-style diet (separate plan from Bez). Re-check HbA1c every 3 months.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="moderate-tag">8</span></td>
    <td><b>Vitamin B6 toxicity</b><br>B6 = 435 nmol/L (range 51–183) — 2.4× the upper limit<br>B1 = 220 (range 78–143)</td>
    <td>This is supplement overdose, almost certainly. B6 above ~200 nmol/L causes peripheral neuropathy (tingling, numbness, balance issues) and the damage can be permanent if it goes on for years.</td>
    <td>Stop ALL B-complex supplements immediately. Check what's in your multivitamin and any "energy" or "nerve" supplements — many contain 100+ mg of B6. Re-test B6 in 3 months. Resume only if level normalizes and only at &lt;25 mg/day.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class="moderate-tag">9</span></td>
    <td><b>Low free testosterone</b><br>Free T 0.159 nmol/L (target &gt;0.225)<br>Total T 10.2 nmol/L (low-normal)</td>
    <td>Borderline hypogonadism. Contributes to fatigue, low HDL (yours has been 32–39 for years), muscle loss, reduced libido. Worth a full hormone workup.</td>
    <td>Endocrinology referral. Get full panel: Total T (AM), Free T, SHBG, LH, FSH, Estradiol, Prolactin. If confirmed low, TRT is increasingly considered safe in selected men. NOT the first thing to fix — fix cardiac first.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="section">🟢 The Good News — Don't Lose Sight of This</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Finding</th><th>What It Means</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Ejection Fraction 60–70%</b></td><td>Excellent heart pump function. Your stent did its job; the muscle is healthy.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Stress test 9.1 METS</b></td><td>Above-average exercise capacity. Equivalent to running comfortably. Strong protective marker.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Bone density (Sheba DXA)</b></td><td>Femoral neck T-scores +0.7 to +1.2 — above normal. FRAX 10-year fracture risk 3.3%. No osteoporosis concerns.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Cognitive scores</b></td><td>Global 98, Memory 96, Executive 92, Attention 107 — all in normal range. Brain is solid.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>PSA 3.03 (May 2026)</b></td><td>In age-adjusted normal range. Free/Total ratio 0.21 from Dec 2023 was reassuring. Continue annual.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Vitamin D 62.9 ng/mL</b></td><td>Excellent — in the longevity-optimal 40–60+ range.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Kidney filtration eGFR 80</b></td><td>Filtration is preserved despite the albuminuria — meaning early-stage kidney disease, reversible with proper treatment.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Liver enzymes normal</b></td><td>ALT 13, AST 24 (May 2026) — statin tolerating well, no fatty liver signal.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="section">📋 Recommended Action Sequence (Next 30 Days)</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Week</th><th>Do This</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>This week</b></td><td>(1) Stop all B-vitamin supplements. (2) Get a home BP cuff if you don't have one and measure morning+evening for 7 days. (3) Call cardiology for a visit specifically to discuss LDL/ApoB/Lp(a)/aorta.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Week 2</b></td><td>Cardiology visit. Bring this document. Push for: Crestor titration or PCSK9, ARB+SGLT2 stack, 6-month aortic surveillance schedule, ApoB and Lp(a) repeat labs.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Week 3</b></td><td>GI visit for H. pylori treatment. Confirm urea breath test or stool antigen post-treatment.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Week 4</b></td><td>PCP visit: review medication changes, order repeat labs in 8 weeks (lipid panel, ApoB, hs-CRP, HbA1c, UACR, eGFR).</td></tr>
</table>

